  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">HIV and AIDS Home Based Care Project, Kisumu</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">St Francis Community Development Programme (FRACODEP) implements integrated HIV and AIDS home based care (HBC) for people living with HIV and AIDS (PLWHA), and HIV counselling and testing (HCT) projects in three parishes.  The three parishes are Ranga&#x2019;la, St. Paul&#x2019;s Parish and Nyang&#x2019;oma in the Arch-Diocese of Kisumu.  The integration of HCT in HBC response has provided an opportunity for project expansion with the provision of holistic and meaningful benefits to PLWHA, OVCs and the wider community.

FRACODEP&#x2019;s integrated HBC and HCT project evaluation (April 2013) revealed that there was value addition to the project delivery in terms of knowledge of HIV status, behaviour change attributed to reducing new infections, improved physical status of clients who continue to participate in awareness creation and as community HBC volunteers, increased acceptance of HIV status, reduced stigma at individual, family and community level, disclosure, and positive living.  Additionally, infected people are seeking early treatment, and awareness of what to do such as adopting good practices including: drug adherence, use of nutritious foods and sharing of personal experiences.  More people have appreciated and adopted best dietary practices for their improved health following sensitisation that nutrition plays a key role in boosting the health of PLWHA especially those on treatment to ensure drug adherence. The programmes approach in re-integrating PLHIV into economic activities represents a progress towards renewal and revival of socio-economic vibrancy.  The project currently serves over 10,000 people including men, women, youth and children infected with and affected by HIV and AIDS.

During this implementation cycle of one year ending April 2014, this project will continue to provide integrated HBC and HCT services.  It will focus on greater involvement of PLWHA to ensure their visibility.  New clients still in denial will be reached through mobilization and sensitization exercises.  FRACODEP will continue to reach the wider population with HIV and AIDS education in order to influence their attitude and practice towards HIV and AIDS. Huge investment will be put in training and Income Generating Activities (IGA) for active community volunteers who will be retained for improved and quality service delivery, easy manageability, reporting, supervision and monitoring as well as motivation. 

FRACODEP will continue to contribute to CAFODs programme overall goal of preventing the spread of HIV, care and support for people infected and affected by HIV and AIDS. The primary project beneficiaries are PLWHA. Others include the youth and larger local community in the catchment area.</narrative>
  </description>
